WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.494 --> 00:00:10.788
Welcome to the Triage Evidence Worklist. This is a practice demo with made-up patients. In about seven minutes, you'll see everything it does, step by step.

2
00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:18.403
First, choose who you are. Riley is a nurse. Sam is the charge nurse, who makes the final decisions.

3
00:00:19.247 --> 00:00:21.170
We'll start as Riley.

4
00:00:21.181 --> 00:00:26.455
This is the main screen. Everything fits on one page, so there's no long scrolling.

5
00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:37.950
On the left are the pages: Patients, Emergencies, Notes to review, Overdue, Practice tools, and Help. The colored badges show how many things need attention.

6
00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:44.109
Across the top is the system status, plus the Voice button and text-size buttons.

7
00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:49.160
The blue box is your guide. It always tells you what to do next.

8
00:00:50.037 --> 00:01:03.098
This is the care queue. The patient at the top is seen first. The colored edge and badge show how urgent each patient is. Level 1 is the most urgent, and purple means nobody has assessed them yet.

9
00:01:04.007 --> 00:01:09.581
Let's see what happens when a new note arrives. The guide says: send Patient B's new note.

10
00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:23.092
A screen reader user hears a short message with no patient name, so nothing private is spoken out loud. And notice: the care queue did not change. The AI cannot move patients.

11
00:01:23.914 --> 00:01:28.702
Let's open the new note. It opens on the right, and the list stays visible.

12
00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:35.571
The yellow label says AI-assisted, unverified. A person always has to check it.

13
00:01:36.523 --> 00:01:46.959
The first finding quotes the note exactly: Patient B now reports demo signal A. It also found that earlier, the patient denied it. So something changed.

14
00:01:47.795 --> 00:01:56.570
The second finding is about the patient's mother, not the patient, so the AI kept it separate. A simple keyword search would get this wrong.

15
00:01:56.586 --> 00:02:05.530
Under What Claude received, you can see exactly what was sent to the AI: two short excerpts, with no name, no record number, and no dates.

16
00:02:05.543 --> 00:02:12.761
Now record your decision: Accept, Correct, or Reject. We'll accept. Your decision is saved with your name.

17
00:02:12.776 --> 00:02:20.675
Only a charge nurse can change how urgent a patient is. Riley goes back to the list and keeps her place on Patient A.

18
00:02:20.687 --> 00:02:26.284
Now Sam, in another window, approves Level 1 for Patient B. Watch the list.

19
00:02:27.165 --> 00:02:35.001
Patient B moved to the top, but Riley's place stayed on Patient A. Pressing Enter opens Patient A, not someone else.

20
00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:44.756
Next, emergencies. Clear, written rules can flag a possible emergency. Let's send a practice note where Patient D reports a warning sign.

21
00:02:45.568 --> 00:02:54.278
A red banner appears, and the Emergencies badge lights up. A screen reader user hears an urgent alert, still without the patient's name.

22
00:02:55.446 --> 00:02:59.868
A flag never moves a patient by itself. Any nurse can check it.

23
00:02:59.885 --> 00:03:05.115
Open it to see why it was flagged: the rule, and the exact sentence from the note.

24
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:12.367
Riley checks the patient and answers yes or no. Writing a reason is required, so everyone can see why.

25
00:03:12.385 --> 00:03:17.234
Save the check. It's added to the history, and the charge nurse can now see it.

26
00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:27.196
Sam, the charge nurse, reads Riley's reason and makes the final decision: confirmed emergency. Sam can change it again later if things change.

27
00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:36.170
Confirming the emergency moved Patient D to Level 1, and every step is saved with a name, a time, and a reason.

28
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:44.656
Now the patient chart. Like big hospital systems, it has tabs: Summary, Notes and documents, Write note, and Emergencies.

29
00:03:44.674 --> 00:03:48.585
The Notes and documents tab shows every note, newest first.

30
00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:55.047
Use the search bar to find words. Let's search for denies. Every match is highlighted in yellow.

31
00:03:55.061 --> 00:04:01.041
To write a note, open Write note. You can type, or press Start dictation and simply speak.

32
00:04:01.851 --> 00:04:09.878
Notes you write or dictate stay on this server. They're never sent to the AI, and the emergency rules check them automatically.

33
00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.695
Save it, and it appears in the chart with your name and the time.

34
00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:23.112
The Voice button lets you control the app by speaking. Press Start listening and say something like: bring up patient C. You can also type the command.

35
00:04:23.126 --> 00:04:30.130
And Patient C's chart opens. This helps nurses who can't easily use a mouse, or whose hands are busy.

36
00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:37.770
Try other commands too, like take a note for patient B, search for denies, or open emergencies.

37
00:04:37.885 --> 00:04:42.859
Pin any note to keep it on screen as a sticky note while you look at other patients.

38
00:04:42.872 --> 00:04:51.069
The Overdue page shows late tasks and who is responsible. If someone runs late, a backup is assigned automatically.

39
00:04:51.082 --> 00:04:56.209
What if the AI stops working? Here we pretend it's down, and send another note.

40
00:04:57.057 --> 00:05:03.525
The note still shows up, marked manual review required. Everything else keeps working without the AI.

41
00:05:03.582 --> 00:05:10.096
Need a reminder of what a word means? Open Help and words. And use the A buttons to make text bigger.

42
00:05:10.115 --> 00:05:20.167
That's the tour. Remember: the AI suggests, and people decide. To practice again, open Practice tools and press Reset all demo data.

